ts³11099706420³1version³13³1cache_oldmajor³120³1cache_oldauthor³120³1revision³121³1cache_diff_default_author³11³1tscreate³11074450020³1text_default³1ip³2217.247.136.68³2ts³21099706420³2version³21³2name³2text_default³2data³2summary³3*³3text³3= <nowiki>AnCap</nowiki> Theory =

:<nowiki>AnCap</nowiki> Theory generally concerns itself with:

::1. justification or effective modes of argumentation
::2. prediction of what an <nowiki>AnCap</nowiki> society would be like
::3. possible implementation stategies

:For prediction of what an <nowiki>AnCap</nowiki> society would be like, see HowItWorks.

:The other concern, justification or effective modes of argumentation, generally has two approaches:

::1. [[Utilitarian]]
::2. [[Natural Law]]

:Then each approach attempts to deal with the two bases of <nowiki>AnCap</nowiki>:

::1. [[Non-Aggression Principle]] (NAP, or sometimes Zero-Agression Principle, ZAP)
::2. [[Private Property]]

:That leads to six base areas of theory and theoretical development:

::1. [[Utilitarianism and AnCap]]
::2. [[Natural Law and AnCap]]
::3. [[Utilitarianism and NAP]]
::4. [[Utilitarianism and Private Property]]
::5. [[Natural Law and NAP]]
::6. [[Natural Law and Private Property]]

:While that covers many of the current topics and controversies commonly seen today, there are still many difficulties in the basic philosophy underlying these issues.

::[[Basics of Philosophy and AnCap]]
::[[Justification]]
::[[Fallacies of Argumentation]]
::[[Complaints and Defenses]]


